rotmgtradenode design doc
=====================

Two pieces: the NODE (rotmgtradenode, a self-hosted item pool each player
runs on their own accounts from their own computer) and the HUB
(rotmgtradehub, a website that lets nodes find each other, swap items, and
share a communism). The hub runs no bots, never logs into the game, holds no
items, no credentials and no money, and sees only what nodes publish: their
offers and their communism (6.3).
Everything physical happens on players' own accounts from their own
machines. Both are open source. A third, private service that supplies
finished accounts to a node is designed separately and is not part of this
document.


1. Goals and non-goals
----------------------

Goals
  - every player can run their own pool from their own computer, as promised
    in the shutdown notice, with no dependency on a central fleet
  - the project cannot be taken down by one ban wave: no central roster, no
    central accounts, no central IP
  - nodes that opt in can trade with each other and pool items in a communism
  - a node owner can set accounts aside as a communism, so anyone on the hub
    can deposit and withdraw there without running the app, like the
    original rotmgcommunism pool
  - accounts are the owner's own alts, made on Realm's site and played through
    the tutorial by hand before they join a node

Non-goals (for now)
  - no bots on the hub of any kind: no reserve accounts, no escrow, no /tell
    listener, and no Realm traffic at all
  - no market and no points: no currency, no fractional decas, no credit or
    contribution ledger. Cross-node trades are item for item; communism is
    free and capped
  - no raids, realm hunting, leaderboard, skin redeeming, or the dev API on the
    node


2. Ban-risk model (hard constraints)
------------------------------------

What the Sept 2026 wave showed: bots that only traded were ~2% banned. The
recycle lane (delete + recreate characters) and the scheduled mass-login lane
were what got the old cohort banned. So every node must obey:

  - a few accounts per player, logging in only
    through proxies the player pastes into the node (one account per exit IP
    at a time; the "proxy only" rule is on by default, so no proxies means
    no logins). A direct login from the home IP is an explicit opt-out
  - no character recycling, ever
  - no scheduled logins across accounts beyond the once-a-day login a node
    gives an account with a backpack day still ahead on its calendar; bots log
    in when the owner uses them
  - the hub never logs into the game; every game login is a node's, through
    the owner's own proxies
  - a Realm build change must not cause a mass kick: nodes check the current
    protocol before logging in (section 8)

Any future feature that breaks one of these needs its own risk write-up first.

Risk write-up, trades with players (6.2b, 2026-09-23): a stranger on the hub
can make one of a node's pool bots log in and stand in a nexus for up to
thirty minutes, which bends "bots log in when the owner uses them". Kept
small: off by default, a per-node cap on meetings at once, one meeting per
person at a time and six an hour, a day's pause after two no-shows, the bot
invites a player at most every two minutes and otherwise waits for their
/trade, and every login still goes through the owner's proxies and the
build gate. Communism accounts already meet strangers the same way (6.3).


3. Trust model
--------------

  - the hub is trusted by nodes. The node's source is public so players can
    see what runs on their machine and what it sends the hub
  - nodes are NOT trusted by the hub or by each other. A node runs on a player's
    machine and can lie about anything it reports
  - the only thing two nodes can prove to the hub is that they agree: a trade is
    real when both nodes sign the same receipt
  - a node is identified by a keypair it generates on first run. Logging in
    to the hub from the client links that key to a hub user account (4.3). The
    hub knows node public keys and never any credentials
  - a node verifies its own owner locally by /tell to the owner's own bot. The
    hub does not verify player IGNs on its own; it records bot IGNs and lets
    counterparties attest them (see 6.2). The operator may name one node the
    login node (6.6): its /tell desk proves characters for the hub, and the
    hub trusts it with that because the operator runs it
  - a trade with a player (6.2b) has one witness, the node: its receipt alone
    closes it, the trade window being all or nothing for both sides. What the
    player reports afterwards reaches the operator and freezes nobody


4. Components
-------------

4.1 Node (rotmgtradenode)
  - open source. Fork of rotmgcommunism `rewrite`, trimmed to: the pool,
    deposit/withdraw, /tell owner login, inventory tracker, trade machine,
    backpack claim and use per account
  - cherry-pick branch `rotmg-trade` (offers between nodes, item locking)
  - Electron app: tray shell around the existing Hono server + Vite SPA,
    auto-update, credentials in the OS keychain, SQLite under the user data dir.
    Windows first, code-signed; Linux and Mac builds if cheap. better-sqlite3
    needs an electron-rebuild step
  - opt-in ban telemetry (section 8)
  - works fully standalone (see 4.4). Hub connection is optional and off by
    default
  - roster: the owner's own alt accounts, added by hand or delivered by the
    separate account service (a pickup client, not built)
  - accepted items: the catalog is every item the game lets players trade
    (not Soulbound in equip.xml); each node chooses what its bots take in
    (rules by category and lowest tier per gear group, per-item pins), kept
    in its settings and enforced in the trade window and in every site path
    that takes an item in. What is already in the pool stays withdrawable
  - account storage (docs/relay/STORAGE.md): each account's vault chests,
    potion rack, Gift Chest and seasonal spoils chest are cold storage the
    owner moves items into and out of from the console; a run walks the
    account into the Vault and does the moves as INVSWAPs. Items in storage
    are not in the pool until taken out. The owner also picks which
    character an account logs in with. Characters trade with 8, 16 or 24
    slots (the upgraded backpack has 16)

4.2 Hub (rotmgtradehub)
  - open source. A website + API. No bots, no items, no credentials, no
    money, no Realm traffic. Every secret it uses is env, not code
  - the website is the one human interface for anything cross-node: a
    front page with live numbers and the download, a dashboard of live
    node cards and a first-run checklist, the offer board and communism
    with buttons that queue requests for nodes (anyone: deposits into and
    withdraws from a node's communism; the owner: offers, accepts, cancels,
    communism takes and gives with their own node), an activity feed pushed
    over server-sent events, and Discord webhook notifications. Items render as tiles from the node package's catalog
    and sprite sheet; wants are built with a picker. Sign-in is Google or
    Discord; nodes link with one-time codes
  - holds: hub user accounts, node registry (public key, bot IGNs, version,
    last seen), offers, communism accounts and listings, the request queue,
    trade receipts
  - ban telemetry store (section 8)
  - hosting: a small site anywhere (Railway or a Vultr VPS)

4.3 Node modes and hub login
  Local mode (default, no hub account needed)
    - everything in 6.1 works: roster, pool, deposit/withdraw, /tell owner
      login, backpacks per account, all state in the local SQLite
    - the app never contacts the hub except for the version check, which can
      be turned off
    - no hub features: no offers, no communism across nodes, no hub requests

  Connected mode (link the node to a hub account)
    - hub accounts are made on the hub website by signing in with Google;
      the hub keeps the email address and a display name. This is a hub user
      account, separate from any game account, and it has no password
    - the website hands out a one-time link code (my nodes -> link a node:
      eight characters, fifteen minutes). The owner pastes it into the node's
      console; the node sends its public key with the code and the hub links
      the key to the account. One hub account can have several nodes
      (desktop + laptop); each node is listed on the hub with its bot IGNs and
      last-seen time, and can be unlinked from the website
    - after linking, hub requests are signed with the node key; no hub
      secret is ever stored on the node, and the hub revokes the key by
      deleting the node row from the website
    - logging in unlocks: posting and accepting offers, communism across
      the hub (taking from and giving to other nodes' communism with my bots,
      and other people using mine), and hub-side status (which of my nodes
      are online, open offers, communism items)
    - logging out or losing the hub does not touch local state. Open offers
      and communism listings for that node are hidden on the hub while the node
      is offline and resume when it reconnects. Locked items stay locked
      locally until the node hears the offer's final state
    - the roster and all credentials stay local in both modes; connected mode
      only adds a signed channel to the hub

4.4 Wire contract
  - node -> hub requests are signed with the node key, same shape as the
    existing HMAC-signed HttpSiteApi
  - the hub speaks the SiteApi-style pull model: a node asks "what should I do",
    the hub answers with an assignment


5. Data ownership
-----------------

Node SQLite (never leaves the machine)
  - accounts and passwords, roster, per-bot capacity
  - tracked item instances (instance UUID, item id, enchant record, holder bot)
  - the request queue, offer state mirror

Hub database
  - hub user accounts (email, Google subject or Discord id; no passwords) and the nodes
    linked to each
  - nodes, bot IGN attestations
  - offers (give / want, status, expiry)
  - each node's communism: its communism accounts (room per half) and their items
  - the request queue: what hub users asked a node to do, and the node's
    progress reports
  - trade receipts
  - opt-in ban telemetry: per node, per account hash: suspended-at, last
    seen, last activity kind, node version, Realm build
  - current Realm build + protocol ids, for the node pre-login check

Item identity across nodes: instance UUIDs are minted per node tracker, so a
delivered item gets a new UUID on arrival. The hub matches items by item id +
enchant record, never by instance id.


6. Flows
--------

6.1 Owner deposit / withdraw (node only)
  - same as rotmgcommunism: the owner logs in by /tell and trades with their
    own bot; everything on the pool accounts is theirs. No hub involvement

6.2 P2P swap (node <-> node via hub)
  - a node posts an offer: give N items (locked, max 24 = 8 inventory + 16-slot
    backpack per trade window), want M items with per-slot enchantment filters.
    The items are the owner's pool items (never communism's)
  - another node accepts; the hub checks both sides have room and creates a
    rendezvous: server, both bot IGNs, both item lists, a time window
  - both nodes run the consolidation swap path (consolidate_give /
    consolidate_take with swapItems): each bot checks the other's offer
    against the assignment before accepting. The trade window is atomic, so
    no escrow is needed
  - larger swaps are chunked across windows; each completed window is its own
    receipt
  - each node signs a receipt (rendezvous id, window index, items seen leaving
    and arriving, TRADEDONE result) and sends it. The hub marks the window
    done only when both receipts match
  - each receipt also attests the counterparty's bot IGN as seen in the trade
    window; the hub counts attestations per IGN
  - abort rules: peer not online within the window, server usage gate red,
    trade window fails, receipts disagree -> offer returns to open, locked items
    unlock, both nodes told why. Disagreeing receipts freeze both nodes' open
    offers until reviewed
  - trade limits: a new node may have 1 open offer and small windows; limits
    grow with matching receipts

6.2b Trades with players (a person without a node, 2026-09-23)
  - a node that allows it (setting, off by default) lets anyone signed in to
    the hub take its offers with their own character: Trade in game on the
    board. The hub makes the meeting at once (rendezvous kind `player`); the
    node's bot brings the offer's items to the server's nexus; the page tells
    the person when to /trade it, and what the node sees
  - in the window the bot puts its items up and judges theirs against the
    offer's want lines (enchantment filters included); it holds off and says
    why while they do not fit, accepts only after them, and a window that
    closes without the trade can be tried again until the deadline
  - the node's receipt closes it; its count of trades with players is apart
    from completed swaps (which drive the offer limits). The person can call
    it off, confirm, or report a problem
  - caps and the risk write-up: section 2

6.3 Communism (federated, no points)
  - a node sets whole accounts aside for communism ("communism" flag on the
    roster). Their trade slots are communism's room, everything on them
    (character slots and storage) is a communism item; the pool is everything
    else. The fleet never uses a communism account for pool work
  - the node publishes its communism to the hub: accounts (IGN, half, slots,
    free, online) and items (ref = instance id). The hub shows one federated
    board: every online node's items, and each node's room per half
  - anyone signed in to the hub can deposit into a node's communism and take
    from it, as much as they like, no points, no caps: they give their IGN
    and a server, the hub queues a request for that node, and one of its
    communism accounts meets them in game like the original rotmgcommunism
    pool. The node reports progress (queued, which bot to /trade, done)
  - node-to-node: a node's own bot can take a listed item onto its pool
    (communism account gives, the taker's bot receives, nothing back) or
    give pool items into another node's communism (the giver's bot gives, a
    communism account of the target with room receives). Same receipt protocol
    as 6.2: recorded only when the giver's "gave" equals the taker's "got"
    and the taker gave nothing. Taking an item unlists it at once; a failed,
    aborted or expired meeting lists it again
  - the operator takes it all back at any time: unflag the account and it
    is a pool account again with whatever it holds; or withdraw communism
    items to their own character like anyone else

6.4 Account supply
  - outside this document. A node takes only accounts that have played
    through the tutorial already.

6.5 Hub requests
  - the hub keeps one queue of what its users asked a node to do: communism
    deposits and withdraws by anyone, and the owner's own offers, accepts,
    cancels, communism takes and gives. The node polls it, runs each request
    against its queue or coordinators, and posts results: progress notes
    while a row is open (a bot has claimed it: "/trade <bot>"), then done or
    failed. A request nobody takes within 30 minutes expires
  - no grants, no quotas, no per-user storage on a node: what a hub user can
    reach is exactly communism, and only a node's owner acts with its bots
  - local mode has no hub requests; the owner's own communism still works from
    the node's page


6.6 Signing in with a Realm character (the login node, 2026-09-23)
  - the operator names one node the login node (admin page). People sign in
    to the hub, or prove the character they trade with, by whispering a code
    from that character to the node's login desk bot; the node reports who
    whispered it. No email, no password: an account made this way has the
    character's name and nothing else
  - once a login node is set, communism deposits and withdraws and trades in
    game go by proven characters only; a character is proven on one account
    at a time (whoever whispered last)

7. Node scope: keep / cut from rotmgcommunism
---------------------------------------------

Keep: GameClient, protocol codecs, inventory tracker, trade machine,
per-bot capacity, chunked withdraw, /tell login, the pool UI, backpack claim
and use (manual, per account), Realm server list refresh at boot.

Nothing from the old account generator's registration code stays on the node.

Cut: backpack scheduler lanes (recycle, daily login, audit), raids, realm
hunting, pool leaderboard, skin redeem, dev API, ban sweep,
server usage reporting to a site (replaced by a node-local check).


8. Versioning, protocol safety, telemetry
-----------------------------------------

Versioning
  - the hub publishes a minimum node version; older nodes get read-only hub
    access until updated
  - Realm build changes (protocol.json from the him.is feed) ship as node
    updates through Electron auto-update
  - a node must keep working with the hub down: all hub calls fail soft

Protocol-break safety
  - a Realm patch that changes packet ids kicks every bot that logs in with
    stale codecs, and a synchronised kick across every node is a ban signal
  - before any game login the node compares its built-in Realm build id and
    protocol hash against the current one. Source, in order: the hub's
    current-build endpoint, the him.is protocol feed, and as a last resort
    the build id reported by Realm's own app-init/version endpoint
  - mismatch = the node refuses to log in, tells the owner "Realm updated,
    waiting for a rotmgtradenode update", and keeps checking for the auto-update.
    Local mode does the same using the feed directly
  - the hub flips its current build as soon as the operator confirms the new
    protocol, and the node release that carries it goes out through
    auto-update

Server list refresh
  - the node fetches Realm's server list at boot and on every login gate
    rather than shipping a static IP table, so a server move never strands a
    node

Opt-in ban telemetry
  - off by default; the client asks once, with a plain explanation of what
    is sent
  - when on, the node reports to the hub: a salted hash of each account
    (never the email), suspended-at when a login returns the ToS suspension
    text, last seen, what the account was doing last (idle, owner trade,
    swap, communism, backpack job), node version, Realm build, and account age
    bucket
  - the hub joins this across nodes into the same view scripts/ban-pattern.py
    gave for the fleet, so a wave is visible while it is starting, split by
    activity lane and cohort, and the operator can post a warning or flip
    the minimum version to pause a risky feature
  - telemetry is never used for anything but this and the aggregate is what
    gets published, not per-node data


9. Phasing
----------

  1. Node standalone: the pool, deposit/withdraw, /tell login, server list refresh, protocol pre-login check
     against the feed, Electron build, signed Windows installer. This is the
     shutdown-notice promise and ships first
  2. Hub v0: hub user accounts, log in from the client, node linking, node
     registry, version feed, current-build endpoint, ban telemetry intake
  3. P2P swaps: offers, rendezvous, receipts, attestations, trade limits
  4. Communism: communism accounts, the federated board, deposits and withdraws
     by anyone on the hub, node-to-node takes and gives. (Replaced, on
     2026-09-21, the first cut of opt-in items with a daily cap, and the
     personal vaults and shared vaults that came with it.)
  5. Account supply: separate private service, not part of this document
  6. Later, if ever: an operator-run reserve fleet, modelled as just another
     node with a trusted flag so bringing it back is config, not protocol


10. Open questions
------------------

  - where the hub is hosted (Railway or Vultr)
  - how a player without any alt gets a first account
  - telemetry salt: per node (unlinkable across nodes) or hub-wide (lets the
    hub spot the same account on two nodes)
  - whether receipts should include a packet-level proof (last TRADE packets)
    or just the node's summary
  - whether a node's communism should get any protection against being
    emptied by one person (today it is the operator's own choice of accounts
    and nothing else)
  - one character slot per account means no seasonal/non-seasonal switching
    without deletion, which rule 2 forbids; decide per-account seasonality at
    creation and never change it
